# Frontend Design Reference Rules

## Design Reference Process

When the user provides a URL to a Hero section or any other design reference section, follow this process:

### 1. Fetch and Analyze Design Reference

- Use `mcp_web_fetch` or `web_search` tools to retrieve the design reference from the provided URL
- Analyze the HTML structure, CSS styles, and visual design elements
- Extract all relevant design tokens (see Token Extraction section below)

### 2. Extract Design Tokens

Extract and document the following design elements from the reference:

#### Typography
- Font families (primary, secondary, monospace)
- Font sizes (heading levels, body text, captions)
- Font weights (light, regular, medium, bold, etc.)
- Line heights
- Letter spacing

#### Colors
- Primary colors (brand colors, accent colors)
- Secondary colors
- Text colors (headings, body, muted)
- Background colors (primary, secondary, overlays)
- Border colors
- Any gradients or color transitions

#### Spacing & Layout
- Padding and margin values
- Container max-widths
- Grid or flexbox patterns
- Gap values between elements

#### Visual Effects
- Border radius values
- Box shadows (if any)
- Transitions and animations
- Opacity values

#### Component-Specific Styles
- Button styles (sizes, variants, hover states)
- Input field styles
- Link styles
- Image treatment (aspect ratios, object-fit)

### 3. Store Design Tokens

- Update `frontend/design-tokens.ts` with all extracted tokens
- Use TypeScript interfaces for type safety
- Organize tokens by category (typography, colors, spacing, etc.)
- Document the source URL in comments

### 4. Apply Design Consistently

**CRITICAL**: All pages and components must follow the same design language as the reference section.

- Use the extracted design tokens consistently across all components
- Maintain the same typography scale throughout the application
- Use the same color palette everywhere
- Apply consistent spacing patterns
- Keep the same visual style (modern, minimal, bold, etc.)

### 5. Implementation Pattern

- Update `tailwind.config.ts` to include custom colors, fonts, and spacing from design tokens
- Use CSS variables in `globals.css` for dynamic values if needed
- Reference design tokens in components via Tailwind classes or CSS variables
- Ensure responsive design follows the same visual language

## Example Workflow

1. User provides: "https://example.com/hero-section"
2. Fetch the URL using web tools
3. Analyze and extract:
   - Font: Inter, sizes: 48px (h1), 24px (h2), 16px (body)
   - Colors: #1a1a1a (text), #0066ff (primary), #f5f5f5 (background)
   - Spacing: 64px sections, 24px between elements
   - Border radius: 8px
4. Update `design-tokens.ts` with all values
5. Update `tailwind.config.ts` to include these values
6. Apply these tokens to ALL pages and components

## Consistency Checklist

Before completing any frontend work, verify:
- [ ] Typography matches the reference design
- [ ] Colors match the reference palette
- [ ] Spacing follows the reference patterns
- [ ] Visual style is consistent across all sections
- [ ] Components use the same design language

## Notes

- If multiple reference sections are provided, prioritize the first one as the primary design language
- If design tokens conflict with existing styles, replace existing styles to match the reference
- Always maintain accessibility (contrast ratios, readable font sizes) while following the design reference
